00:00:45.609 --> 00:00:49.899
Ok, quick warning: there are spoilers ahead,
so if you've not seen the movie yet, why not

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:52.519
come back here after you've seen the film!

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:56.260
When Tim and Boss Baby's parents have to go
on a work-trip to Las Vegas, they leave their

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.490
children at home with their new babysitter,
Eugenia, who's actually really Francis E.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:04.010
Francis's brother Eugene dressed up in women's
clothes.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:09.210
This part of the film heavily references Disney's
1964 classic movie Mary Poppins.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.030
Just look at Eugenia's clothing, including
her little hat and flower, the way Francis

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.710
E Francis calls her 'practically perfect in
every way', the way Boss Baby refers to her

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.650
as 'Scary Poppins', and also the way she flies
through the air using her umbrella.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:27.680
As Eugenia aka Eugene chases them down the
street, Boss Baby tells Tim he must cycle

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.910
faster if they're going to escape, at which
point the movie takes us inside Tim's mind

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:36.360
where he imagines himself and Boss Baby sailing
on a stormy sea as they're pursued by an enormous

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.460
whale with the face of Eugenia!

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.190
This scene tips its hat to the 1956 film Moby
Dick and the classic novel on which it was

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.190
based.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.820
Also, during that chase scene through the
streets, Tim imagines himself and Boss Baby

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.580
on a motorcycle jumping through a ring of
fire over a moving train, which is a little

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.930
tribute to the many airborne stunts of the
legendary daredevil performer, Evel Knievel.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.380
If you were thinking that Tim's talking alarm
clock, Wizzie the wizard, sounds rather familiar,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.040
that's because actor James McGrath who voices
him is particularly skilled at celebrity impressions,

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:10.359
and based Wizzie's voice on actor Ian McKellen,
who famously played Gandalf in Peter Jackson's

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:11.999
Lord of the Rings and Hobbit movies.

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:16.049
And, the line that Wizzie says every morning,
'Wake up, little halflings!', is also a nod

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:21.170
to the novels of JRR Tolkien in which 'halflings'
is another name for 'hobbits'.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.769
Director Steven Spielberg's iconic action-adventure
movies, including the Indiana Jones films,

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:29.670
were an inspiration for the Boss Baby filmmakers,
particularly when it came to creating and

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.889
editing the fantasy sequences dreamt up by
Tim.

00:02:32.889 --> 00:02:37.560
So, when Tim is running through a jungle,
flying a spaceship, or fighting a gorilla,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.010
the movie's editor channelled the editing
style used in Spielberg's classic movies.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.330
But whip-cracking archaeologist Indiana Jones
wasn't the only character that inspired Tim's

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.290
wild flights of imagination.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.390
The filmmakers also took cues from the Looney
Tunes cartoon character Ralph Phillips, who,

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.099
like Tim, was a young boy with a vivid imagination
and a tendency to daydream.

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:01.090
Likewise, the movie's graphic, stylised look
is also an homage to Warner Brothers' Looney

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:05.920
Tunes cartoons, and several Disney films from
the golden age of animation influenced the

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:06.920
movie too.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.680
For example, the fantasy scene where Tim saves
his parents from inside a shark by using hot-sauce

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.959
is a riff on Disney's 1940 animated film Pinocchio,
where Pinocchio starts a fire to escape from

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:21.329
the huge whale Monstro that swallowed him
and his father Geppetto.

00:03:21.329 --> 00:03:24.989
Another of director Steven Spielberg's movies
also left its mark on the neighbourhood where

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:26.639
Tim's family lives.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:31.060
According to The Boss Baby director Tom McGrath,
they set the movie in small-town suburbia

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.639
in a past that combines the 1970s and 80s,
and for the appealing look of Tim's neighbourhood,

00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:39.489
they took cues from the much-loved early 80s
movie, ET.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:43.900
You probably noticed that the magnetic letters
on the fridge change from Tim's name to 'Baby'

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.840
and that Tim's photo gets covered over with
baby pictures once Boss Baby arrives.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.779
But what you might not have spotted is that
before Boss Baby arrives, Tim is wearing a

00:03:51.779 --> 00:03:53.829
T-shirt with the number '1' on it.

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:58.209
However, just after Tim spots Boss Baby arriving
in a taxi, Tim doesn't change his clothes

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:02.730
but the number on his T-shirt does change
from '1' to '2' as he rushes down the stairs

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.709
to find out what's going on, which of course
signals that he no longer feels like number

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:09.120
one priority in the family.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.590
During a big action scene where Tim chases
Boss Baby and his toddler team around the

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.820
garden as Boss Baby drives a toy cop car,
there's a musical hat tip to the theme tune

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.980
of the 1970s TV series S.W.A.T., which followed
the escapades of a Special Weapons and Tactics

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:22.980
team.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:27.450
And the movie's action scenes also make use
of sound effects from the popular 1970s sci-fi

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:31.760
action series, The Six Million Dollar Man,
which starred actor Lee Majors as a special

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.990
agent with bionic implants that gave him superhuman
abilities.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:40.030
And, of course, because the character of Boss
Baby is brought to life by actor Alec Baldwin,

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.400
the movie includes homages to roles he's played
on the big and small screen.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.880
For instance, during one of Boss Baby's meetings
with his Special Mission Team of Staci, The

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.940
Triplets, and Jimbo, there's a lovely nod
to another demanding businessman Baldwin played

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.970
in the 1992 movie Glengarry Glen Ross.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.730
In that film, his character uttered the now-famous
lines "Put that coffee down.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.400
Coffee's for closers".

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.980
And in this movie that becomes: "Put that
cookie down.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.760
Cookies are for closers", which Boss Baby
says to Jimbo when he's telling him off for

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.070
eating cookies before they've succeeded in
their mission.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:16.510
The character of Boss Baby also clearly plays
on Alec Baldwin's award-winning turn as executive

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:21.080
Jack Donaghy in the critically acclaimed TV
comedy 30 Rock, to the point that the movie's

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.800
business-savvy baby is pretty much a mini-Jack-Donaghy
in diapers, barking orders, spouting management

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.440
advice, and talking about corporate retreats.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.490
And just like 30 Rock's Donaghy who idolises
his boss Don Geiss, wanting to follow in his

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:39.430
footsteps and be promoted to the top job when
Geiss retires, so too Boss Baby admires his

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:43.290
manager, and is striving for a promotion and
a corner office of his own.

